# devtools / devtools-dev.
#
# One false positive matched four times: trivy's node-pkg analyzer reads
# VS Code's bundled extension manifest
# usr/share/code/resources/app/extensions/npm/package.json (name "npm",
# version "1.0.1" — the extension's own) and takes it for the npm CLI at
# 1.0.1. No npm CLI is installed in this image. Re-test on a trivy bump.
CVE-2018-7408
CVE-2019-16775
CVE-2019-16776
CVE-2019-16777

# CVE-2022-25235 / CVE-2022-25236: the Ubuntu snap-stub "firefox" epoch
# false positive documented in apps/firefox/.trivyignore (Firefox comes
# from the desktop baseline).
CVE-2022-25235
CVE-2022-25236

# CVE-2026-102422 — NOT a false positive: shell-quote 1.10.0 (arbitrary
# command execution through crafted input, fixed in 1.11.0) vendored
# inside VS Code itself,
# usr/share/code/resources/app/node_modules/shell-quote. The version is
# whatever the Microsoft APT repo ships; this image pins the repo and
# its signing key, not VS Code's bundled tree, so the fix arrives with a
# VS Code release.
#
# Accepted because it crosses no privilege boundary here: VS Code reaches
# shell-quote when building terminal/task command lines out of the user's
# own workspace config, and the user of a devtools session already owns
# an unrestricted shell in that container (devtools-dev even ships sudo,
# HARDENING.md § Reduced profile). "Arbitrary command execution as the
# session user" is this image's baseline capability, not an escalation —
# and the pod stays unprivileged, read-only-rootfs, caps dropped.
# Re-test on every VS Code bump; drop the line as soon as the bundled
# copy reaches 1.11.0.
CVE-2026-102422
